{ignore}
<p><code>{mail to=$email, template='order', subject='Order confirmation'}</code> renders
<code>_mail/order.pad</code> to the HTML part and <code>_mail/order.txt</code> to the text part -
made from the HTML when there is no <code>.txt</code> - after running
<code>_mail/order.php</code>, inside the <code>_inits.pad</code>/<code>_exits.pad</code> of that
<code>_mail/</code> with <code>&#64;page&#64;</code>. The template is looked up like a snippet, from the
page's directory up to the application and <code>_common</code>.</p>

<p>As a pair without <code>template=</code> the rendered content is the HTML part - inside a
loop with the fields of the row, one mail per occurrence. The tag shows nothing; the mail sees
the page's variables and leaves them as they were. <code>from=</code> (default
<code>$padMailFrom</code>), <code>cc=</code>, <code>bcc=</code> and <code>replyTo=</code> are the
other headers.</p>

<p><code>$padMailTransport</code> decides where the mail goes: <code>'file'</code> (the
default) writes an <code>.eml</code> under <code>DATA/mail/&lt;app&gt;/</code>, keeping the
newest <code>$padMailKeep</code> - no mail server needed; <code>'mail'</code> sends through PHP's
<code>mail()</code>; the name of an application function gets the message array and answers
whether it went. A line break in a header value, an address that is none, a missing subject or
template are errors. From PHP: <code>padMail ( $to, $template, $subject, $vars, $options )</code>,
and <code>[ 'queue' =&gt; TRUE ]</code> sends it through the job queue.</p>

<p>The tag sends mail, so its examples are shown, not run.</p>
{/ignore}
